<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Merged into the bundled Info.plist by the macOS bundler.

  This file exists for one reason: a Hearth that runs on another machine is
  reached over plain http and ws on the home network, and macOS refuses both
  from a WKWebView unless the app says otherwise.

  The house speaks http by design. It listens on a LAN and on a tailnet, where
  there is no certificate authority to issue it anything and no name it could
  present one for -- "192.168.1.20" is not a domain. Turning that into https
  would mean shipping a certificate story before there is a product, so the
  transport is plain and the CONTENT is what is protected: the gateway refuses
  every off-machine request that cannot present a device token.

  NSAllowsArbitraryLoads rather than NSAllowsLocalNetworking, because the
  address people will actually type is as often a tailnet name as a LAN
  literal, and the narrow exemption covers only the second. Both are the same
  house; a client that reached one and not the other would be a product with a
  hidden asterisk.

  NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription is the sentence macOS shows when it asks
  whether this app may talk to other machines on the network. It should say
  what the app wants, in the words of the person being asked.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
  <dict>
    <key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
    <true/>
  </dict>
  <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
  <string>Hearth needs the local network to reach a Hearth running on another computer in your home.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
